    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Lovettsville began in 1732 as “The German Settlement,” founded by Pennsylvania Germans who farmed the rich Loudoun Valley topsoil.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        16,
        17,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitloudoun.org/towns-and-places/lovettsville/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1828 the community was renamed Lovettsville after landowner David Lovett, who had subdivided his property into quarter‑acre lots in 1820.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lovettsville%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Virginia General Assembly officially chartered Lovettsville as a town in 1836, but full incorporation waited until 1876.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lovettsville%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During World War II, a DC‑3 airliner crashed in Lovettsville in 1940, killing 25 people and becoming known as the Lovettsville air disaster.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lovettsville%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Lovettsville’s historic district, featuring 19th‑century brick and clapboard homes, was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 2012.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
